/*
 * LC 347 — Top K Frequent Elements
 *
 * PROBLEM:
 * Given integer array nums and integer k, return k most frequent elements.
 * Answer can be in any order.
 *
 * CONSTRAINTS:
 * - 1 <= nums.length <= 10^5
 * - -10^4 <= nums[i] <= 10^4
 * - k is always valid — 1 <= k <= unique element count
 * - Follow-up: solve in better than O(n log n)
 *
 * CORE INSIGHT:
 * Step 1 always same — build freq map: num → count
 * Step 2 differs — how to extract top k efficiently
 *
 * ALL APPROACHES:
 * ┌──────────────┬────────────────────────────────────┬───────────┬──────┐
 * │ Approach     │ Idea                               │ TC        │ SC   │
 * ├──────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┼───────────┼──────┤
 * │ Brute Force  │ Build freq map, sort by count desc │ O(n log n)│ O(n) │
 * │ Min Heap     │ Maintain heap of size k            │ O(n log k)│ O(n) │
 * │ Bucket Sort  │ Freq as index, scan right to left  │ O(n)      │ O(n) │
 * └──────────────┴────────────────────────────────────┴───────────┴──────┘
 *
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 1 — BRUTE FORCE (Sort by frequency)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY: Simplest — build freq map, sort entries by count descending.
 * HOW: Build freq map → sort entrySet by value descending → take first k.
 *
 * TC: O(n log n) — sorting dominates
 * SC: O(n)       — freq map + sorted list
 * REJECTED: Follow-up explicitly asks to beat O(n log n)
 */
public int[] topKFrequentBrute(int[] nums, int k) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
    for (int num : nums)
        freq.put(num, freq.getOrDefault(num, 0) + 1);
    // sort entries by frequency descending
    List<Map.Entry<Integer, Integer>> entries = new ArrayList<>(freq.entrySet());
    entries.sort((a, b) -> b.getValue() - a.getValue());
    int[] result = new int[k];
    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++)
        result[i] = entries.get(i).getKey();
    return result;
}

/*
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 2 — MIN HEAP (Intermediate)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY Min Heap not Max Heap:
 * Max heap would require popping all n elements to get k → O(n log n)
 * Min heap of size k — keeps only k largest seen so far.
 * When heap exceeds size k, poll() removes smallest → only top k remain.
 *
 * HOW:
 * Build freq map → push all entries into min heap ordered by frequency
 * If heap size > k → poll() removes lowest frequency entry
 * At end heap contains exactly k most frequent elements
 *
 * TC: O(n log k) — n insertions each costing O(log k)
 * SC: O(n)       — freq map O(n) + heap O(k)
 *
 * WHY better than sort:
 * Sort → O(n log n) — sorts ALL elements
 * Min heap → O(n log k) — only maintains k elements, k <= n
 */
public int[] topKFrequentHeap(int[] nums, int k) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
    for (int num : nums)
        freq.put(num, freq.getOrDefault(num, 0) + 1);
    // min heap — ordered by frequency ascending
    Queue<Map.Entry<Integer, Integer>> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(
        Comparator.comparingInt(Map.Entry::getValue)
    );
    for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {
        pq.offer(entry);
        // maintain heap size k — remove lowest frequency
        if (pq.size() > k) pq.poll();
    }
    int[] result = new int[k];
    int i = 0;
    while (!pq.isEmpty())
        result[i++] = pq.poll().getKey();
    return result;
}

/*
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * APPROACH 3 — BUCKET SORT (Optimal)
 * ─────────────────────────────────────────
 * WHY BUCKET SORT:
 * Key insight — max possible frequency of any element = n (all same).
 * Frequencies range from 1 to n → create bucket array of size n+1.
 * bucket[i] = list of numbers appearing exactly i times.
 * Scan right to left → naturally gets highest frequency first → O(n)!
 *
 * WHY n+1 size:
 * Index 0 unused (frequency 0 meaningless).
 * Index n = element appearing n times (entire array same element).
 * Valid indices 1 to n → size n+1 covers all cases.
 *
 * WHY List[] not int[]:
 * Multiple elements can have same frequency → same bucket.
 * Example: [1,1,2,2,3] → freq{1→2, 2→2, 3→1}
 * bucket[2] = [1,2] ← both in same bucket!
 *
 * CRITICAL — iterate freq.entrySet() NOT nums to fill buckets:
 * Iterating nums causes duplicates in buckets:
 *   nums=[1,1,1,2,2,3] → bucket[3] gets 1 added THREE times!
 *   Result picks same element multiple times → wrong output.
 * entrySet() has one entry per unique number → no duplicates.
 *
 * DOUBLE BREAK PATTERN:
 * Inner break stops inner loop when k reached.
 * Outer loop condition `untilK < k` stops outer loop.
 * Both needed — inner break alone lets outer loop continue
 * → untilK exceeds k → ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException!
 *
 * Dry run — nums=[1,1,1,2,2,3], k=2:
 * freq: {1→3, 2→2, 3→1}
 * bucket[1]=[3], bucket[2]=[2], bucket[3]=[1]
 * scan right to left:
 *   i=6,5,4: null → skip
 *   i=3: bucket[3]=[1] → result[0]=1, untilK=1
 *   i=2: bucket[2]=[2] → result[1]=2, untilK=2==k → break
 * return [1,2] ✅
 *
 * Same frequency edge case — nums=[1,1,2,2,3], k=2:
 * freq: {1→2, 2→2, 3→1}
 * bucket[2]=[1,2], bucket[1]=[3]
 * i=2: num=1 → result[0]=1, untilK=1
 *      num=2 → result[1]=2, untilK=2==k → break
 * return [1,2] ✅
 *
 * TC: O(n) — freq map O(n) + bucket fill O(n) + scan O(n)
 * SC: O(n) — freq map + bucket array both O(n)
 *
 * Edge cases:
 * - k=1 → stops after first element collected ✅
 * - All same element → bucket[n]=[num] → picked first ✅
 * - All unique → bucket[1]=[all nums] → first k picked ✅
 */
public int[] topKFrequent(int[] nums, int k) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
    for (int num : nums)
        freq.put(num, freq.getOrDefault(num, 0) + 1);
    // bucket array — index = frequency, value = list of nums with that freq
    List<Integer>[] bucket = new List[nums.length + 1];
    for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {
        int count = entry.getValue();
        int num = entry.getKey();
        // lazy init — only create list when needed
        if (bucket[count] == null)
            bucket[count] = new ArrayList<>();
        bucket[count].add(num);
    }
    int untilK = 0;
    int[] result = new int[k];
    // scan right to left — highest frequency first
    for (int i = bucket.length - 1; i >= 1 && untilK < k; i--) {
        if (bucket[i] == null) continue;
        for (int num : bucket[i]) {
            result[untilK++] = num;
            // inner break — stop collecting when k reached
            if (untilK == k) break;
        }
        // outer condition untilK < k handles stopping outer loop
    }
    return result;
}
